        "id": "ch6_4_3-1",
        "text": "Chapter 6 I Advanced Enumeration Phase\n147\nBrute-Forcing a Web Portal Using Hydra\nIn the previous section, we needed to access the Jenkins web portal. Now you\u2019ll\nlearn how to brute-force any web portal using Hydra (not only Jenkins). We can\nfollow this process each time we want to brute-force a web page:\n1.I Open the login page.\n2.I Enable the proxy on Burp and the browser.\n3.I Enter invalid credentials and submit the data (using the submit button).\n4.I Intercept the request using the Burp proxy and send it to the repeater.\n5.I Extract the following four items:\nI\nI URI\nI\nI Username name field\nI\nI Password field\nI\nI Error message\nFigure 6.1: Jenkins Web Portal\nFigure 6.2: Jenkins Error Message\n148\nChapter 6 I Advanced Enumeration Phase\nNOTE Always check the manufacturer site (just Google the model number or web\nportal name) for the default username and password. A lot of web portal admins keep\nthe default credentials, so you don\u2019t have to really brute-force your way in. Here is an\nexample of an attractive website that maintains an inventory of the default credentials:\ndatarecovery.com/rd/default-passwords/ .\nStep 1: Enable a Proxy\nFirst, we need to enable a proxy on the web browser. Take note that the Burp\nproxy will be listening on port 8080 on the Kali host. Don\u2019t get confused with\nthe port 8080 that the Jenkins host web server is using. We can use the Firefox\nbrowser on our Kali host. Open the menu and select Preference, scroll to the\nend of the newly opened window, and click Settings in the Network Settings\nsection, as shown in Figure 6.3.\nNow, select the proxy radio button and make sure to set the following:\nI\nI Set HTTP Proxy to 127.0.0.1.\nI\nI Set Port to 8080 (that\u2019s the port of Burp proxy and not Jenkins).\nI\nI Select the option Use This Proxy Server For All Protocols.\nI\nI Click OK to save the settings.",
